Part 2 — The VRYOH Argument, Built From Verified Evidence
Combining rows 2 and 4 produces a specific, defensible argument unique to VRYOH's model, not available to a generic AI-response tool:
●	Anderson (Cornell) found that responding to more than 85% of all reviews correlates with LOWER revenue than not responding at all.
●	Zhang et al. (IJCHM) found the mechanism behind high-response-rate penalties is response SIMILARITY — templated, robotic replies — not response volume itself.
●	VRYOH's architecture generates a distinct draft per review from the review's actual content, with mandatory human approval before publication — structurally the opposite of the templated-reply pattern this research identifies as the actual driver of the revenue penalty.
This is a two-study, independently-sourced argument: VRYOH is positioned to let a business respond to a high share of its reviews without triggering the penalty this research associates specifically with genericness, not volume.
